  "query_preview": "..\n\nSfc high pressure will strengthen as it expands across the Great \nLakes today through Labor Day amid rising mid level geopotential \nheights across the region. Northeasterly low level winds circulating\naround this sfc high have been advecting a drier airmass across Se \nMi, characterized by sfc dewpoints in the 50s. Mid level subsidence \nwill suppress diurnal mixing depths this afternoon capping afternoon \nhighs in the 70s. Steep low level lapse rates and flow off Lake Huron\nwill also result in a fair amount of diurnal cumulus this afternoon.\nThe influence of the sfc high and mid level ridging will sustain a \ndry forecast through Tuesday. As the sfc high pressure departs across\nthe eastern Great Lakes Labor Day into Tuesday there will be a \ngradual veering of the low level flow from easterly to southerly by \nTuesday, supporting a gradual warming trend. The influence of the sfc\nhigh and broad subsidence will maintain relatively tranquil \nconditions to end the holiday weekend and start the work week. Recent\nrainfall, an easterly component to the surface winds off the lakes \nand nocturnal boundary layer decoupling suggests some semblance of a \nfog potential the next couple of nights. \n\nDamping of the mid level ridge axis over the Great Lakes is forecast \nmid week as a progressive longer wavelength trough originating from \nthe Pacific Northwest drives a cold front across the forecast area \nin the Tuesday night/Wednesday time frame. A narrow axis of deep \nlayer moisture is forecast to advect into Se Mi along/ahead of this \nfront. While strong mid level wind fields will contribute to adequate\ndeep layer shear, GFS/ECMWF ensemble members indicate an overall \nweakly unstable environment across Se Mi. This would potentially \nlimit the thunderstorm and severe weather risk. Shower chances will \nstill remain warranted, with ensemble members suggesting the higher \nrain chances across the northern portions of the forecast area where \nmodels indicate a little better kinematic forcing.",
